Beach Volleyball.

1. High-level concept.
1.1. Working title - Beach Volleyball.
1.2. Concept - 2v2 action beach volleyball tournament against computer bots.
1.3. Genre - Arcade action sport.
1.4. Pillars
  - Fast and physical. Every rally is a short burst of running, jumping and diving.
  - Timing is the skill. Every touch (serve, pass, set, spike, block, pancake) is a timed press with a perfect window.

2. Product design.
2.1. Player Experience
Player controls only one player in the team of 2, against computer bots in a beach volleyball tournament. They run around the sand with WASD, read the ball, and land timed jumping attacks, serves, digs and last-second pancake saves.
2.2. Visual and Audio style.
Sunshine, Beach, Tropical Island. Punchy hit effects: sand bursts on dives, screen shake and a slow-motion beat on a perfect spike.

3. Detailed and Game system design.
3.1. Core loop
Serve -> rally (up to 3 touches per side: pass, set, attack) -> point -> next serve. Match is first to 10 points, rally scoring (every rally gives a point). Winner advances on the tournament ladder.

3.2. Court
Camera follows the player from a 3rd-person view, behind and above, looking toward the net. Characters move freely inside their own half.

3.3. Movement
  - WASD = run in 8 directions inside your own half. Cannot cross the net.
  - Short acceleration and slide-to-stop on sand, so positioning early matters.

3.4. The ball
  - A shadow and a landing marker on the sand always show where the ball will come down.
  - Ball touches the sand on your side = point to the opponent. Ball lands out of the court = point to the side that did not touch it last.

3.5. Actions (all timing-based)
Every action has a timing window: Perfect / Good / Miss. Perfect gives the best arc, speed and accuracy. Good gives a weaker, less accurate version. Miss means a whiff or a shank.

  - Serve - stand behind the baseline, hold the action key to toss and release at the top of the toss.
      Floater serve (standing release): safe, slow.
      Jump serve (jump + release at peak): fast and hard, but a bad release goes into the net or out.
  - Pass / Bump (first touch) - stand under the ball and press as it drops. Sends the ball up to your teammate for a set.
  - Set (second touch) - press under the ball to put up a high, hittable ball near the net.
  - Jumping attack / Spike (third touch) - a two-step timing skill.
      Approach and jump: while running (WASD held), hold Space to charge the jump. The player keeps running while charging. Release Space to take off; a longer hold gives a higher jump (about 0.6 m for a tap, 1.8 m at full charge). The same charge bar as the pass shows the charge.
      Hit: left mouse click in the air swings at the ball. The hand is about 0.5 m above the head. The click is graded by how far it is in time from the ball crossing the hand, using the same Perfect / Good / Miss windows as the pass.
        Perfect = full-speed spike (about 20-25 m/s), straight to the target.
        Good = about 70% speed and a little spread.
        Late Miss = soft tip over the net. Early Miss or ball out of reach = whiff.
      Jump timing matters by itself: the ball is hit wherever the hand is at the click. Taking off too early means falling, so the contact is lower and the spike flatter (net top is 3.0 m, raised above real height to suit the arcade jumps; hand at the top of a full jump is about 4.3 m). Best spike = full charge, on-time take-off, click at the top of the jump.
      Aim: WASD held at the click picks one of 9 targets on the enemy court. W = deep (near the baseline), S = short (just past the net), A / D = left / right sideline, diagonals = corners, no key = middle of the court. The spike is fired at the target, and gravity and drag act on it in flight. The landing marker shows where it really lands.
  - Block - jump at the net as the enemy hitter attacks. Perfect = stuff block straight down for a point. Good = deflection back up on your side.
  - Defence / Dig - receive a hard spike with a timed bump. Harder to time than a normal pass.
  - Dive - when the ball lands out of reach, dive in the WASD direction to extend reach.
  - Pancake - the last-chance save. Dive and slap the hand flat on the sand just as the ball arrives, so the ball pops up off the back of the hand. Very tight timing window. A successful pancake gets a special effect and keeps the rally alive.

3.6. Controls (keyboard)
  - W A S D - Move
  - Space - Action, by context. Standing still (no WASD) on the ground with the ball dropping toward you = pass: hold to charge (longer hold = higher pass), release to hit, WASD at release aims (no direction = straight up), and the character stands still while charging. Otherwise = jump: hold to charge (longer hold = higher jump), release to take off, and the player keeps running while charging (spike approach, block at the net, jump serve).
  - Left mouse click - Hit in the air (spike). WASD at the click aims.
  - K - Dive / Pancake

3.7. Teammate
The player's partner is a bot. Its first job is passing the ball back and forth with the player.
  - Who plays the ball: no double touches. After the player touches the ball, it belongs to the teammate, and after the teammate touches it, it belongs to the player. On a fresh ball, whoever is closer to the landing marker takes it.
  - On its ball: the teammate runs to the landing marker with the same speed and acceleration as the player and plays the ball as it drops to forearm height.
      After the player's touch it sets: a high ball (tunable, about 5 m) to a spot about 1 m from the net in line with the player, so the player can approach and spike.
      On a fresh ball it passes to the player, as before.
  - Off its ball: it walks back to a fixed home spot on its side of the court (tunable).
  - Skill: each touch rolls Perfect / Good / Miss with set odds (default 70 / 25 / 5) and uses the same power and accuracy penalties as the player's pass. A bad roll can shank the ball, and the player then has to chase it.
  - Player's pass assist: if the WASD direction at release points within about 45° of the teammate, the pass goes to the teammate's position. Otherwise it travels its normal distance in the WASD direction. Timing still decides accuracy.

3.8. Opponents
Computer bots use the same moves and timing windows as the player. Tournament opponents get harder by improving their timing accuracy, reaction speed and attack choice.

3.9. Tournament
A ladder of bot teams. Win a match to advance, lose and retry the match. Beat the final team to win the tournament.
